IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate

-- ccc-core.l4 -- the shared vocabulary for the Criminal Code (Canada), R.S.C., 1985, c. C-46.
-- Written by the keel agent. DECLARE and constants only: no rules here.
-- Source: a plain-text rendering, made by a converter script, of the Justice Laws Website consolidation
-- (current to 2026-09-30). It is not the official text, and nothing here is claimed verified against the official text.
--
-- SEAT: a person who is, or may be, accused of an offence under the Code, asking on facts they can state what
-- they did, whether it is an offence, how grave it is, what punishment, what defences, rights and procedures
-- apply from arrest to appeal, and what follows from a conviction.
--
-- USE: a part imports this module and nothing else of mine (`IMPORT `ccc-core``). Types and constructors shared by
-- parts live ONLY here. A part that needs a type this module lacks defines it under its own section prefix
-- (`s 515(6) ...`) and records it in NEEDS-CORE.md. Money is a NUMBER of dollars; a date is `Date day month year`.
-- Membership test used for what is in this module: could a witness of ordinary competence testify to the fact,
-- or does answering it require reading the Act? Only the first kind is here, plus the nouns every answer is made of.

§ `Where: the provinces and territories`

-- src:182 | superior court of criminal jurisdiction means
-- src:183 | (a) in the Province of Ontario, the Court of Appeal or the Superior Court of Justice,
-- src:184 | (b) in the Province of Quebec, the Superior Court,
-- src:185 | (c) in the Provinces of Nova Scotia, British Columbia, Prince Edward Island and Newfoundland and Labrador, the Court of Appeal or the Supreme Court,
-- src:186 | (d) in the Provinces of New Brunswick, Manitoba, Saskatchewan and Alberta, the Court of Appeal or the Court of Queen’s Bench,
-- src:187 | (e) in Yukon and the Northwest Territories, the Supreme Court, and
-- src:188 | (f) in Nunavut, the Nunavut Court of Justice; (cour supérieure de juridiction criminelle)
@ref s 2 "superior court of criminal jurisdiction" (the thirteen provinces and territories, as the text names them) (src:182-188)
DECLARE `Province or territory` IS ONE OF
    Ontario
    Quebec
    `Nova Scotia`
    `British Columbia`
    `Prince Edward Island`
    `Newfoundland and Labrador`
    `New Brunswick`
    Manitoba
    Saskatchewan
    Alberta
    Yukon
    `Northwest Territories`
    Nunavut

§ `Who: every person or body the text puts a power, duty, liberty or liability on`

-- One actor type, so that every part's regulative rules (PARTY ... MUST/MAY/SHANT ...) range over the same cast.
-- The courts are here too, because a court acts as a party. Names are the Code's own words.
@ref Criminal Code s 2 (offender, victim, complainant, peace officer, public officer, justice, provincial court judge, prosecutor, Attorney General, counsel, clerk of the court, representative, senior officer, organization, Review Board)
DECLARE Party IS ONE OF
    `the accused`                                -- the individual in the seat: charged, or liable to be charged, with an offence
    `the offender`                               -- the same person once determined guilty: s 2 "offender"
    `the victim`                                 -- s 2 "victim"; s 2 "complainant" means the victim
    `a witness`
    `a juror`
    `the jury`
    `counsel for the accused`
    `the prosecutor`                             -- s 2 "prosecutor"
    `the Attorney General`                       -- as s 2 defines it, which varies with the proceeding
    `the Attorney General or Solicitor General of the province`   -- s 2 "Attorney General" para (a)
    `the Attorney General of Canada`
    `the Director of Public Prosecutions`
    `the informant`                              -- whoever lays an information
    `a peace officer`                            -- s 2 "peace officer"
    `a public officer`                           -- s 2 "public officer"
    `a justice`                                  -- s 2 "justice": a justice of the peace or a provincial court judge
    `a provincial court judge`
    `a judge`                                    -- a judge of the court that the provision in question names
    `the court`                                  -- the court the provision in question names
    `a court of criminal jurisdiction`           -- s 2 "court of criminal jurisdiction"
    `a superior court of criminal jurisdiction`  -- s 2 "superior court of criminal jurisdiction"
    `a court of appeal`
    `the Supreme Court of Canada`
    `a Review Board`                             -- Part XX.1
    `the clerk of the court`
    `a surety`
    `a probation officer`
    `a supervisor`                               -- supervises a conditional sentence or a long-term supervision
    `the keeper of a prison`
    `the Correctional Service of Canada`
    `the Parole Board of Canada`
    `the Minister of Justice`
    `the Governor in Council`
    `an organization`                            -- s 2 "organization"
    `a representative of an organization`        -- s 2 "representative"
    `a senior officer`                           -- s 2 "senior officer"

§ `What: the kind of offence, the kind of proceeding, the decisions and sentences`

-- "Hybrid" and "dual procedure" do not occur in the text. A section that gives both limbs says
-- "is guilty of (a) an indictable offence ... or (b) an offence punishable on summary conviction".
@ref Criminal Code Part XXVII (summary convictions) and the penalty sections throughout
DECLARE `Mode of proceeding` IS ONE OF
    `by indictment`
    `on summary conviction`

DECLARE `Class of offence` IS ONE OF
    `an indictable offence`
    `an offence punishable on summary conviction`
    `an offence that the section makes both indictable and punishable on summary conviction`

-- The kinds of decision the Code asks a court (or a justice) to make about the charge itself.
@ref Criminal Code ss 2 (offender, unfit to stand trial), 548, 579, 606, 672.31, 672.34, 730
DECLARE `Decision on the charge` IS ONE OF
    `a finding of guilt`
    `an acquittal`
    `a verdict of not criminally responsible on account of mental disorder`
    `a verdict of unfit to stand trial`
    `a stay of proceedings`
    `a withdrawal of the charge`
    `a discharge at the preliminary inquiry`
    `an order to stand trial`

-- The kinds of homicide offence: a result that Part VIII gives (it classifies) and that sentencing, parole-ineligibility and appeal
-- provisions then turn on, so it is one type here rather than one in each part.
@ref Criminal Code ss 222, 229, 231, 232, 234, 236, 745
DECLARE `Kind of homicide offence` IS ONE OF
    `first degree murder`
    `second degree murder`
    `manslaughter`
    `infanticide`

@ref Criminal Code Part XXIII (sentencing)
DECLARE `Kind of sentence` IS ONE OF
    `an absolute discharge`
    `a conditional discharge`
    `a fine`
    `imprisonment`
    `a conditional sentence of imprisonment`
    `an intermittent sentence`
    `probation`
    `restitution`
    `forfeiture`
    `a prohibition order`
    `a victim surcharge`

@ref Criminal Code Part XVI (compelling appearance of the accused and interim release)
DECLARE `Outcome of interim release` IS ONE OF
    `release on a release order without conditions`
    `release on a release order with conditions`
    `release on a release order with conditions and a surety`
    `detention in custody`

-- The accused's journey, so that a question can be filed under the stage the person is at.
DECLARE `Stage of the proceedings` IS ONE OF
    `investigation and arrest`
    `compelling appearance and interim release`
    `election, plea and pre-trial procedure`
    `preliminary inquiry`
    `trial`
    `sentencing`
    `appeal`
    `after sentence`

-- The documents the Code names as the means by which a person is brought to court, kept out of custody or held.
@ref Criminal Code s 2 (appearance notice, recognizance, release order, summons, undertaking, indictment, count)
DECLARE `Process document` IS ONE OF
    `an information`
    `an indictment`
    `a count`
    `a summons`
    `a warrant for arrest`
    `an appearance notice`
    `an undertaking`
    `a release order`
    `a recognizance`
    `a subpoena`
    `a search warrant`

§ `How much: periods of time, punishments, and where in the Code`

-- A period as the text writes it. "Two years less a day" is years 2, months 0, days 0, `less a day` TRUE:
-- do not turn periods into a day count, because years and months are calendar units and a day count is wrong across leap years.
@ref Criminal Code penalty sections throughout (30 sites write "two years less a day")
DECLARE `Length of time` HAS
    years         IS A NUMBER
    months        IS A NUMBER
    days          IS A NUMBER
    `less a day`  IS A BOOLEAN

DECLARE Imprisonment IS ONE OF
    `no imprisonment is stated`                  -- the limb or section states none (not the same as none being allowed)
    `imprisonment for life`
    `imprisonment for a term of` HAS `the term` IS A `Length of time`

DECLARE Fine IS ONE OF
    `no fine is stated`
    `a fine in the discretion of the court`
    `a fine of` HAS dollars IS A NUMBER

-- One limb of a penalty provision: what the section says for ONE mode of proceeding.
-- A section that has an indictable limb and a summary limb is answered with two Punishments.
DECLARE Punishment HAS
    `mode`                   IS A `Mode of proceeding`
    `maximum imprisonment`   IS AN Imprisonment
    `minimum imprisonment`   IS AN Imprisonment
    `maximum fine`           IS A Fine
    `minimum fine`           IS A Fine

-- Where in the Code. `section` is the section number as printed, as a decimal (380; 83.02; 320.1001), which sorts in the
-- Code's order, so "sections 83.18 to 83.23" is a range test; no section number ends in a zero after the point.
-- `subsection` is "" or the subsection as the Code writes it, for example "(2.01)".
DECLARE `Provision of the Code` HAS
    `section`      IS A NUMBER
    `subsection`   IS A STRING

-- Periods the penalty sections use again and again (counts in the text: two years 83, five years 76, ten years 63, fourteen years 47).
--                          years months days less a day
`six months`              MEANS `Length of time` OF 0, 6,  0, FALSE
`ninety days`             MEANS `Length of time` OF 0, 0, 90, FALSE
`two years less a day`    MEANS `Length of time` OF 2, 0,  0, TRUE
`two years`               MEANS `Length of time` OF 2, 0,  0, FALSE
`five years`              MEANS `Length of time` OF 5, 0,  0, FALSE
`ten years`               MEANS `Length of time` OF 10, 0, 0, FALSE
`fourteen years`          MEANS `Length of time` OF 14, 0, 0, FALSE
